{"cid": "UgxQqrffOzYmDc9T2KB4AaABAg.94kmAXtMt8i9H2qexDBVCP", "text": "That's actually the preferred way to do things, as the lead is unbroken. The alternative is to use a jumper wire on the other side or the same side, but there's always a chance that an overheat will cause the joint to melt and the wire will fall off.\nWhat I found interesting was to use solder(and not jumper wire) to bridge adjacent nodes. Much cleaner that way. Uses a lot of solder though.", "time": "5 years ago", "author": "@CST1992", "channel": "UCzHTY1vtgWDeL_Ha2m8d1jA", "votes": "0", "replies": "", "photo": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/jGMJub47bhFEFAwsT8JzUpl2sAF1mFasG8gGV8vqqxOryJjozq0r14ulXeAXKBFsG9OYlFgz1Q=s88-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj", "heart": false, "reply": true, "time_parsed": 1609194539.156293}
